 =#
 
 """
+    _expand_compact_array_differential(x)
+
+Rewriter primitive (see [`EXPAND_COMPACT_ARRAY_DIFFERENTIALS`](@ref)) for a single node
+`x`: if `x` is an array-shaped, unexpanded `Differential` application -- e.g.
+`Differential(t, 1)(p(t))`, the WHOLE-ARRAY compact form Symbolics' `compact_dot_derivative`/
+`compact_norm_derivative` (see Symbolics' `diff.jl`) produce when differentiating a
+`dot`/`norm` of an array variable -- rewrite it to `array_literal` of the same
+`Differential` applied to each SCALAR component instead, e.g.
+`array_literal(D(p(t)[1]), D(p(t)[2]), D(p(t)[3]))`. Returns `nothing` (meaning
+"no change") for every other node, per the `SymbolicUtils.Rewriters` convention.
+
+This produces the SAME canonical scalar shape that `fullvars`/`dummy_sub`/`total_sub`'s
+substitution keys already use everywhere else in this file (e.g. `dd = fullvars[dv]`
+just below, or the `neweq.lhs` registered into `total_sub` in
+`generate_system_equations!`). Without this rewrite, a scalar substitution rule for
+`D(p(t)[i])` can never match inside the compact whole-array `D(p(t))` term: they are
+different, though mathematically equal, syntactic shapes (index and unexpanded
+differentiation commute, but nothing canonicalizes that automatically), so a plain
+`substitute` silently no-ops and the compact `Differential` survives all the way to
+codegen, where `ModelingToolkitBase.check_operator_variables` correctly (if confusingly)
+rejects it as a raw operator that was never isolated to an equation's LHS.
+
+A single post-order pass also correctly unwinds a DOUBLY-compact term like
+`D(D(p(t)))` (the compact second derivative Pantelides produces when re-differentiating
+an already-once-differentiated `dot`/`norm` equation): the inner `D(p(t))` is rewritten
+to `array_literal(D(p(t)[i])...)` first (post-order visits children before parents), so
+by the time the outer `D(...)` is visited, indexing into that `array_literal` folds back
+to the plain scalar `D(p(t)[i])`, and re-wrapping it in the outer `Differential` merges
+via the existing same-variable `Differential` composition rule into `Differential(t,
+2)(p(t)[i])` -- exactly the scalar second-derivative shape `fullvars` uses for it.
+"""
+function _expand_compact_array_differential(x)
+    SU.iscall(x) || return nothing
+    op = SU.operation(x)
+    op isa Differential || return nothing
+    args = SU.arguments(x)
+    arr = args[1]
+    SU.is_array_shape(SU.shape(arr)) || return nothing
+    scal_args = Symbolics.SArgsT()
+    sizehint!(scal_args, (length(arr)::Int) + 1)
+    push!(scal_args, Symbolics.SConst(size(arr)))
+    for i in SU.stable_eachindex(arr)
+        push!(scal_args, op(arr[i]))
+    end
+    return Symbolics.STerm(SU.array_literal, scal_args; type = SU.symtype(x), shape = SU.shape(x))
+end
+
+"""
+    EXPAND_COMPACT_ARRAY_DIFFERENTIALS
+
+A `SymbolicUtils.Rewriters.Postwalk` rewriter built from
+[`_expand_compact_array_differential`](@ref); expands every compact, array-shaped
+`Differential` application anywhere in an expression to `array_literal` of scalar
+`Differential`s, so that scalar `D(x)`-keyed substitution rules (`dummy_sub`, `total_sub`)
+can subsequently match them. See `_expand_compact_array_differential` for why this is
+needed and why one post-order pass suffices even for nested/higher-order derivatives.
+"""
+const EXPAND_COMPACT_ARRAY_DIFFERENTIALS = SU.Rewriters.Postwalk(_expand_compact_array_differential)
+
+"""
+    expand_compact_array_differentials(eq::Equation)
+
+Apply [`EXPAND_COMPACT_ARRAY_DIFFERENTIALS`](@ref) to both sides of `eq` and return the
+resulting `Equation`.
+"""
+function expand_compact_array_differentials(eq::Equation)
+    return EXPAND_COMPACT_ARRAY_DIFFERENTIALS(eq.lhs) ~ EXPAND_COMPACT_ARRAY_DIFFERENTIALS(eq.rhs)
+end
+
